translation:
I lean long on the tower, the wind a whisper thin;
I gaze to the far edge—spring sorrow, dim,
Rises from the rim of the world.

Grass fades in mist and the last of the light;
No one can read my silence, my railing-bound plight.

I vow to drink wild, to seek one drunken night,
To sing before the cup—yet forced joy is no delight.
My belt grows loose, yet I would not repent;
For her, I gladly wear myself away.

