Wind howls fierce beneath a vaulted sky, and gibbons cry in grief;
Islets lie clear, white sand gleams, and birds wheel back in flight.
Endless leaves rustle down from boundless boughs;
The ceaseless Yangtze rolls on, surging on.
Ten thousand miles of autumn sorrow, ever a wanderer;
A lifetime of infirmity—I climb the terrace alone.
Hardship and bitter regret have frosted thick my temples;
Worn low, I have but newly set aside my cup of turbid wine.

