translation:
The nation is shattered, yet mountains and rivers remain;
Spring comes to the city, where grass and trees grow thick and wild.
Grieving the times, flowers seem to draw out my tears;
Hating parting, birds startle my heart with their cries.
Beacon fires have burned for three months unbroken;
A letter from home would be worth ten thousand gold.
I scratch my white hair, and it grows ever thinner—
scarcely enough now to hold even a hairpin.

