I lean against the high railing, the fine wind blowing;
Looking far into spring sorrow, darkening at the horizon’s edge.
In the fading light, grass and misty haze blend together;
Silent, who understands my meaning as I rely on the rail?

I plan to be unrestrained, seeking a drunken stupor;
To sing with wine in hand, but forced joy has no taste.
My robe grows loose, yet I regret nothing in the end;
For her sake, it is worth wasting away until I am haggard.

